{"abstention":null,"candidates":[{"claim":"Coverage debt grows superlinearly (likely O(n²)) with rivalry size because each new accepted rival must be criticized against all existing positions and vice versa, while batch criticism capacity grows at most linearly in batch count — so the fraction of pairs ever criticized drops as rivalry grows, making retirement vanishingly rare.","counterconditions":["If batch criticism uses adaptive batch sizing proportional to N², coverage debt could remain bounded.","If the system uses transitive retirement (retiring r_i because it shares a warrant with a refuted r_j), coverage need not be pairwise.","If most rival positions are quickly found equivalent or inequivalent by cheap programmatic checks before expensive criticism, effective N stays small."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"09ffabcd92979168","quote":"# Pass 1: attack (Dung grounded extension; unique, skeptical, polynomial)"},{"block":"0974daebd1665965","quote":"- **Closure rule:** any attacker of `validity_node` attacks the warrant (hence its carrier's attack edge). Enforce in `att` construction."},{"block":"1b2d5ddadda17971","quote":"*Flags:* **lineage stagnation** = conjecture-stream contraction ∧ flat progress (no status changes, no problems retired, no oracle delta where present) sustaine"}],"mechanism":"Each accepted rival position r_i creates criticism obligations against every other accepted position r_j (per-target warrants). With N rivals, there are N(N-1)/2 ordered criticism pairs. If batch criticism processes B pairs per cycle, the number of cycles needed to achieve full coverage is N(N-1)/(2B), growing quadratically. Meanwhile, new rivals are accepted at some rate λ_accept, adding 2N new pairs per new rival. If λ_accept · N(N-1)/2 > B, coverage debt grows without bound. The evidence blocks describe per-target warrants and batch criticism, and the Dung grounded extension (block 09ffabcd92979168) is skeptical — it only retires what is successfully attacked, but if most pairs are never criticized due to debt, almost nothing gets retired.","typicality":0.85},{"claim":"The retirement rate is near zero because the blocking rule (block 174dcccf9f7813e6) only blocks relapse onto refuted-equivalents, and near-duplicates of accepted artifacts are never blocked — so rivals that are superficially different survive even when they are substantively equivalent, preventing criticism from retiring them.","counterconditions":["If the equivalence check B(a) is strengthened to capture substantive (not just syntactic) equivalence, near-duplicate rivals would be blocked at generation time.","If criticism can successfully attack rivals on their own merits (not via equivalence), the blocking rule's permissiveness doesn't matter.","If the variator's inequivalence filter is actually capturing substantive differences, rivals may genuinely deserve to survive."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"174dcccf9f7813e6","quote":"Blocking occurs **only** for relapse onto refuted-equivalents. Near-duplicates of *accepted* artifacts are never blocked — they register normally; the scheduler"},{"block":"0efee9d35ff76f87","quote":"s(a) = Pr[ a' passes B(a) ∧ a' ≉_B a ] # inequivalent survivors only"},{"block":"15134e0610653477","quote":"The sharpest question the spec asks about itself: if you take away every program check and let the loop run on LLM criticism alone (λ = 0), do outcomes actually"}],"mechanism":"The variator emits 'inequivalent survivors only' (block 0efee9d35ff76f87), meaning it explicitly filters for candidates that pass the blocking check B(a) and are not equivalent to a under B. The blocking rule only triggers for 'relapse onto refuted-equivalents,' not for near-duplicates of accepted artifacts. So the generation pipeline actively produces rivals that evade the retirement mechanism. The criticism mechanism must then independently discover that a new rival is substantively equivalent to an already-refuted position, but it has no programmatic help doing so — it relies on LLM criticism (block 15134e0610653477 asks whether outcomes survive with λ=0). The result: generation produces inequivalent-but-substantively-similar rivals faster than criticism can prove their equivalence and retire them.","typicality":0.78},{"claim":"Schools sharing frozen provider seats with conditioning-only differences (block 1958b39701612057) cannot produce genuinely divergent epistemic commitments, so rival positions are numerous but shallow — they vary in surface framing rather than in testable predictions, making criticism unable to find decisive disagreements to exploit.","counterconditions":["If conditioning is strong enough to produce genuinely different reasoning styles even on the same base model, schools could produce divergent predictions.","If different schools use different endpoint families (not just different stance seeds), model-level diversity could produce genuine disagreements.","If criticism can attack on grounds other than differential predictions (e.g., internal coherence), schools need not diverge to be criticizable."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1958b39701612057","quote":"A **school** is a persistent conditioning regime for γ-calls: `(endpoint/family, stance_seed, lineage exemplar slice, render weights)`, registered as a school-p"},{"block":"104e65041f02d759","quote":"Three layers + guardrails. Correction from v1: Def 4.2 coupling and Prop 4.1 reach are **not** the same parameter — coupling is a property of γ, reach a measure"},{"block":"0f670de69de9021d","quote":"`conjecturer (Verbalized-Sampling contract, §11.6), argumentative_critic, defender, variator(µ, µ_struct), judge(rubric verdicts under trial protocol), summariz"}],"mechanism":"A school is defined as '(endpoint/family, stance_seed, lineage exemplar slice, render weights)' — a conditioning regime. If all schools use the same frozen provider (same base model), the conditioning only changes stance and rendering, not the underlying reasoning capacity. This means rival positions tend to share the same blind spots and converge on similar reasoning patterns. Criticism, which needs to find a decisive disagreement between a target and a rival to retire one, cannot find such disagreements because the rivals don't make differentially falsifiable predictions. The coupling (block 104e65041f02d759) between schools is high — they share the same model's inductive biases — so reach (a measure of how far criticism can propagate) is low.","typicality":0.72},{"claim":"The 'downgrade then audit' procedure (block 0c74fc8b6042157a) implies that criticism should first downgrade a rival's confidence before attempting full retirement, but the current system appears to apply criticism as all-or-nothing retirement attempts — no partial credit, no confidence degradation — so positions that should be downgraded survive at full confidence and accumulate.","counterconditions":["If the system already implements confidence degradation outside the criticism mechanism (e.g., via judge rubric scores), the binary criticism outcome is not the bottleneck.","If the Dung extension's skeptical semantics already provide enough discrimination (accept only unattacked), downgrading is redundant.","If the 'downgrade then audit' procedure is already implemented in the harness as described, this claim is vacuous."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"0c74fc8b6042157a","quote":"**The epistemologically principled procedure is \"downgrade, then audit.\"** This is where the harness earns its keep: it already contains the instruments that ce"},{"block":"09ffabcd92979168","quote":"# Pass 1: attack (Dung grounded extension; unique, skeptical, polynomial)"},{"block":"0974daebd1665965","quote":"- **Closure rule:** any attacker of `validity_node` attacks the warrant (hence its carrier's attack edge). Enforce in `att` construction. - **Closure extension"}],"mechanism":"Block 0c74fc8b6042157a states the principled procedure is 'downgrade, then audit' — the harness contains instruments to certify downgraded confidence. But if the criticism mechanism only has a binary retire/survive outcome (per the Dung grounded extension, which is skeptical and unique — block 09ffabcd92979168), then positions that receive criticism but don't meet the bar for full refutation survive at full status. Over time, the accumulation of 'criticized but not retired' positions grows monotonically. A graded confidence mechanism would let the system track that some positions are weakened even if not fully retired, and prioritize future criticism accordingly.","typicality":0.65},{"claim":"The problem is not that criticism is weak but that the acceptance bar for rivals is too low: the variator's inequivalence filter (block 0efee9d35ff76f87) accepts anything that is merely inequivalent under B, without requiring differential testability or explanatory value, so 60 positions survive because generation has no quality gate beyond 'not blocked.'","counterconditions":["If the system's goal is maximal diversity for robustness (ensemble logic), a low acceptance bar may be intentional.","If criticism is supposed to be the quality gate (not generation), adding a generation-side gate would short-circuit the epistemic process.","If differential testability is hard to verify programmatically, the acceptance criterion may be infeasible."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"0efee9d35ff76f87","quote":"variator emits k bounded edits {a'} via µ(·|a) s(a) = Pr[ a' passes B(a) ∧ a' ≉_B a ] # inequivalent survivors only HV_B(a) = 1 - s(a)"},{"block":"174dcccf9f7813e6","quote":"Blocking occurs **only** for relapse onto refuted-equivalents. Near-duplicates of *accepted* artifacts are never blocked — they register normally; the scheduler"},{"block":"0f670de69de9021d","quote":"`conjecturer (Verbalized-Sampling contract, §11.6), argumentative_critic, defender, variator(µ, µ_struct), judge(rubric verdicts under trial protocol), summariz"}],"mechanism":"The survival function s(a) = Pr[a' passes B(a) ∧ a' ≉_B a] only checks that a candidate passes blocking and is inequivalent. There is no requirement that the new rival makes a different prediction, covers a different case, or provides a better explanation. This is a generation-side weakness: the school system produces rivals that are different enough to pass the inequivalence filter but not different enough to be worth criticizing. The fix would be to add an acceptance criterion at generation time: a new rival must either (a) cover a case the existing set doesn't, (b) make a different prediction, or (c) provide a strictly better explanation. This would cap the effective rivalry size and make criticism tractable.","typicality":0.68},{"claim":"Criticism-weighted attention creates a positive feedback loop that starves criticism of budget: as rivals accumulate, attention is spread thinner across more targets, each target receives less criticism, fewer retirements occur, more rivals accumulate — a death spiral where the attention weight per target decreases as 1/N while the criticism work per target stays constant or grows.","counterconditions":["If criticism-weighted attention concentrates budget on high-value targets (not uniform), the death spiral may not occur.","If the attention mechanism has a minimum per-target allocation, budget starvation is bounded.","If generation naturally slows as the problem space saturates (fewer novel positions to find), the equilibrium may be stable."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1b2d5ddadda17971","quote":"*Flags:* **lineage stagnation** = conjecture-stream contraction ∧ flat progress (no status changes, no problems retired, no oracle delta where present) sustaine"},{"block":"1958b39701612057","quote":"A **school** is a persistent conditioning regime for γ-calls: `(endpoint/family, stance_seed, lineage exemplar slice, render weights)`, registered as a school-p"},{"block":"09ffabcd92979168","quote":"# Pass 1: attack (Dung grounded extension; unique, skeptical, polynomial)"}],"mechanism":"If criticism-weighted attention allocates criticism budget proportional to some weight per target, and all targets start with equal weight, then as N grows from 18 to 60, the per-target criticism budget drops by a factor of ~3.3x. This means each target receives less criticism per cycle, so the probability of a successful retirement per target per cycle drops. Meanwhile, the generation pipeline keeps adding rivals. The system reaches an equilibrium where generation rate exceeds retirement rate, and N grows unboundedly. The lineage stagnation flag (block 1b2d5ddadda17971) — 'no problems retired' sustained — would fire, but the system doesn't have a mechanism to halt generation when criticism can't keep up. The fix is to gate generation on coverage debt: don't accept new rivals until the existing coverage debt falls below a threshold.","typicality":0.75}],"context_request":null,"research_proposals":[{"purpose":"Fetch Wikipedia page on ensemble methods to determine whether diversity-generation in ensembles has mechanisms for controlling the number of base learners (analogous to controlling rival count) and whether ensemble pruning literature addresses the problem of too many similar hypotheses surviving.","request_identifier":"wiki_ensemble_methods","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ensemble_learning"]},{"purpose":"Fetch Wikipedia page on peer review to compare how real-world peer review systems handle reviewer load as submission volume grows, and whether there are known mechanisms (triage, desk reject, batch review) that prevent review debt from growing unboundedly — analogous to criticism coverage debt.","request_identifier":"wiki_peer_review","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peer_review"]}],"scratch_proposal":{"links":[{"from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"Root cause is coverage debt (case a).","relation_hint":"Tournament model is one specific answer to the 'how to retire' question, but it presupposes case (a) — coverage debt — is the root cause. If the root cause is (b) or (c), tournaments are irrelevant.","to_ref":"NEW_002","weakens_when":"Root cause is weak criticism (case b) or genuine correctness (case c)."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Speculative: What if the school system adopted a 'tournament' model instead of pairwise criticism? In a tournament, rivals are grouped into batches of k, and only the best rival per batch survives (by some criterion). This would cap the effective rivalry size at ceil(N/k) per round and provide a natural retirement mechanism. The tournament is different from Dung grounded extension because it's comparative (only one survives per batch) rather than absolute (survives if unattacked). Risk: tournaments can eliminate genuinely complementary positions.","possible_next_move":"Design a tournament batch size k that trades off diversity loss against criticism tractability. Could be tested in the simulation by adding a 'tournament' condition.","why_keep_this":"This is a bold alternative to the current criticism mechanism that directly addresses the retirement problem. Worth keeping as a speculative direction even if it has risks."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"content":"Unresolved: The problem says 'criticism retired almost none' but doesn't specify whether this is because (a) criticism attempts are too few (coverage debt), (b) criticism attempts are made but don't succeed (weak criticism or strong defenses), or (c) positions are genuinely not criticizable (they're all correct). These three cases have completely different fixes: (a) needs more criticism budget or better batching, (b) needs stronger criticism or weaker defense, (c) means the system is working as intended and retirement isn't needed. The simulation addresses (a) but not (b) or (c).","possible_next_move":"Request evidence blocks about the defense mechanism and the per-criticism success rate to distinguish cases (a), (b), and (c).","why_keep_this":"This distinction is critical for diagnosing the root cause and was not fully resolved by the candidates above."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"What is the actual per-criticism success rate (fraction of criticism attempts that result in retirement) in the live runs where N grew from 18 to 60? If this rate is near zero, the problem is weak criticism; if it's non-trivial but coverage is low, the problem is coverage debt.","related_refs":["09ffabcd92979168","15134e0610653477"]},{"question":"Does the defense mechanism (block 0f670de69de9021d mentions 'defender' as a role) systematically prevent retirements by successfully defending attacked positions? If so, the problem may be that defense is too strong, not that criticism is too weak.","related_refs":["0f670de69de9021d","0974daebd1665965"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[{"declared_assumptions":["Rivals are accepted at a roughly constant rate λ_accept ≈ 2 per cycle.","Each criticism attempt has probability p_retire ≈ 0.15 of successfully retiring the target.","Batch criticism processes B ≈ 20 pair-criticisms per cycle.","Warrant classes are shared among rivals with ~6 classes (for transitive retirement).","Initial N ≈ 18, matching the problem statement.","Coverage debt is approximated as total pairs minus pairs criticized so far.","Generation gating halts new rival acceptance when debt exceeds a threshold."],"discriminating_purpose":"Distinguish whether coverage debt grows superlinearly (quadratic) or linearly with N, and whether adaptive batching or generation gating can stabilize the system. The simulation will show which of the four rival predictions holds under realistic parameter ranges.","hypothesis":"Coverage debt (uncriticized rival pairs) grows superlinearly with rivalry size N under fixed-capacity batched criticism, because the number of criticism obligations grows as O(N²) while criticism throughput grows as O(1) per batch. Specifically, if rivals are accepted at rate λ_accept and criticism processes B pairs per cycle, coverage debt D(t) ≈ λ_accept·N(t)·t - B·t, and since N(t) grows with t, D(t) grows superlinearly. Retirement rate R(t) ≈ p·B/N(t)² where p is per-pair success probability, so R(t) → 0 as N grows.","interpretation_conditions":["Baseline condition should show N growing to near N_max with debt growing superlinearly and total_retired near zero.","Transitive retirement should show higher total_retired and lower final_N than baseline if warrant-sharing is common.","Adaptive batching should keep debt near zero but may be infeasible at large N (B scales as N²).","Generation gating should stabilize N and allow retirement rate to recover above zero.","If debt grows linearly (not quadratically) in baseline, the O(N²) hypothesis is weakened."],"model_source":"import random\n\ndef simulate(N_init=18, N_max=200, lambda_accept=2.0, B=20, p_retire=0.15,\n            use_transitive=False, adaptive_batch=False, gate_generation=False,\n            debt_threshold=50, cycles=500, seed=42):\n    random.seed(seed)\n    N = N_init\n    debt = 0\n    total_retired = 0\n    total_generated = 0\n    history = []\n    rivals = list(range(N_init))  # each rival has a 'warrant_class' for transitive\n    warrant_classes = {i: random.randint(0, 5) for i in rivals}\n    \n    for t in range(cycles):\n        # Generation\n        if gate_generation and debt > debt_threshold:\n            new_rivals = 0\n        else:\n            new_rivals = int(random.gauss(lambda_accept, 0.5))\n            new_rivals = max(0, new_rivals)\n        for _ in range(new_rivals):\n            rid = max(rivals) + 1 if rivals else 0\n            rivals.append(rid)\n            warrant_classes[rid] = random.randint(0, 5)\n            total_generated += 1\n        N = len(rivals)\n        if N == 0:\n            history.append((t, 0, 0, 0, 0))\n            continue\n        \n        # Pairs to criticize\n        n_pairs = N * (N - 1) // 2\n        \n        # Adaptive batch\n        B_eff = min(n_pairs, B * (N // 10) if adaptive_batch else B)\n        \n        # Criticize random pairs\n        retired_this_cycle = 0\n        for _ in range(B_eff):\n            if len(rivals) < 2:\n                break\n            i, j = random.sample(rivals, 2)\n            if random.random() < p_retire:\n                # Retire j (the attacker's target)\n                # Transitive: also retire rivals with same warrant class\n                to_retire = [j]\n                if use_transitive:\n                    wc = warrant_classes[j]\n                    to_retire = [r for r in rivals if warrant_classes[r] == wc]\n                for r in to_retire:\n                    if r in rivals:\n                        rivals.remove(r)\n                        total_retired += 1\n                        retired_this_cycle += 1\n        \n        N = len(rivals)\n        n_pairs_new = N * (N - 1) // 2\n        # Coverage debt: pairs not yet criticized (simplified)\n        debt = max(0, n_pairs_new - B_eff * (t + 1))\n        history.append((t, N, debt, total_retired, total_generated))\n    \n    return history\n\n# Run conditions\nconditions = {\n    'baseline': dict(use_transitive=False, adaptive_batch=False, gate_generation=False),\n    'transitive': dict(use_transitive=True, adaptive_batch=False, gate_generation=False),\n    'adaptive_batch': dict(use_transitive=False, adaptive_batch=True, gate_generation=False),\n    'gen_gate': dict(use_transitive=False, adaptive_batch=False, gate_generation=True, debt_threshold=50),\n}\n\nresults = {}\nfor name, params in conditions.items():\n    h = simulate(**params)\n    final_N = h[-1][1]\n    final_debt = h[-1][2]\n    total_retired = h[-1][3]\n    total_gen = h[-1][4]\n    results[name] = {'final_N': final_N, 'final_debt': final_debt, 'total_retired': total_retired, 'total_generated': total_gen}\n    print(f'{name}: N={final_N}, debt={final_debt}, retired={total_retired}, generated={total_gen}')\n\n# Also trace N and debt over time for baseline\nprint('\\nBaseline trace (t, N, debt):')\nh = simulate(**conditions['baseline'])\nfor t, n, d, r, g in h[::20]:\n    print(f'  t={t}: N={n}, debt={d}, retired={r}, generated={g}')","parameter_definitions":[{"name":"N_init","values_json":"18"},{"name":"N_max","values_json":"200"},{"name":"lambda_accept","values_json":"[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]"},{"name":"B","values_json":"[10, 20, 40]"},{"name":"p_retire","values_json":"[0.05, 0.15, 0.30]"},{"name":"use_transitive","values_json":"[true, false]"},{"name":"adaptive_batch","values_json":"[true, false]"},{"name":"gate_generation","values_json":"[true, false]"},{"name":"debt_threshold","values_json":"50"},{"name":"cycles","values_json":"500"},{"name":"seed_set","values_json":"[42, 123, 456, 789, 1024]"}],"request_identifier":"sim_coverage_debt_001","requested_observables":["final_N: number of surviving rivals at end of simulation","final_debt: coverage debt (uncriticized pairs) at end","total_retired: cumulative retirements across all cycles","total_generated: cumulative new rivals accepted","debt_growth_curve: debt as function of time, to test quadratic vs linear","retirement_rate: retirements per cycle, to test whether it drops to zero"],"requested_seed_set":[42,123,456,789,1024],"rival_predictions":["If coverage debt grows quadratically, the fraction of pairs ever criticized drops below 10% when N exceeds ~40 with B=20 and λ_accept=2/cycle.","If transitive retirement is added (retiring r_i because r_j with shared warrant is retired), effective coverage grows linearly and debt stays bounded.","If batch size B scales as N² (adaptive batching), coverage debt remains near zero regardless of N.","If generation is gated on coverage debt (no new rivals while D > threshold), N stabilizes and retirement rate recovers."],"simulation_mode":"sandboxed_python_v1"}]}